@prefix rdfs: <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .

<https://w3id.org/hto/data/published/group/AVI_AVI> rdfs:label "adults in Japan, PROP taste testing cohort (Aoki et al. 2023, Nutrients 15(10):2415), TAS2R38 AVI/AVI (n=12)" ;
    obo:HTO_0000058 <https://w3id.org/hto/data/published/diplotype/AVI_AVI> ;
    dcterms:source "PMID:37242298" ;
    rdfs:comment "Aoki et al. 2023, doi:10.3390/nu15102415, PMC10222862 (open access; full text fetched and read via eutils efetch db=pmc). Table 2 reports n=12 for AVI/AVI among the 79 subjects who completed PROP taste detection testing by the modified 2AFC procedure with the QUEST method. Section 3.2 (Results) reports the TAS2R38 genotype effect on threshold was significant (one-way ANOVA F(2,76)=163.32, MSE=12.940, p<0.001) and that AVI/AVI differed significantly from both PAV/PAV (p<0.001) and PAV/AVI (p<0.001), but no numeric per-genotype mean/median threshold (in mM or otherwise) is stated anywhere in the article text or its two tables (Table 1: SNP/genotype frequencies; Table 2: demographics only). The only per-genotype threshold values in the paper are shown graphically, on a log scale, in Figure 3 (scatter plot) and Figure 4 (boxplot); those images could not be fetched from this environment (network access is restricted to eutils.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov; www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov, pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov and mdpi.com all refused or redirected). Left threshold_mmol_per_l empty rather than estimate a number from an unreadable log-scale figure. The paper does report, in Section 4 Discussion, that AVI/AVI non-tasters separate from other genotypes at approximately 0.3-0.6 mM (a separation boundary, not a per-group mean) and that PAV/PAV and PAV/AVI are on average tens to fifty times more sensitive than AVI/AVI; the QUEST procedure tested concentrations from 0.002 to 3.2 mM (=mmol/L) in 20 log-spaced steps." .

<https://w3id.org/hto/data/published/group/PAV_AVI> rdfs:label "adults in Japan, PROP taste testing cohort (Aoki et al. 2023, Nutrients 15(10):2415), TAS2R38 PAV/AVI (n=43)" ;
    obo:HTO_0000058 <https://w3id.org/hto/data/published/diplotype/PAV_AVI> ;
    dcterms:source "PMID:37242298" ;
    rdfs:comment "Aoki et al. 2023, doi:10.3390/nu15102415, PMC10222862 (open access; full text fetched and read via eutils efetch db=pmc). Table 2 reports n=43 for PAV/AVI among the 79 subjects who completed PROP taste detection testing by the modified 2AFC procedure with the QUEST method. Section 3.2 (Results) reports the TAS2R38 genotype effect on threshold was significant (one-way ANOVA F(2,76)=163.32, MSE=12.940, p<0.001) and that PAV/AVI differed significantly from AVI/AVI (p<0.001) and from PAV/PAV (p<0.01), but no numeric per-genotype mean/median threshold (in mM or otherwise) is stated anywhere in the article text or its two tables (Table 1: SNP/genotype frequencies; Table 2: demographics only). The only per-genotype threshold values in the paper are shown graphically, on a log scale, in Figure 3 (scatter plot) and Figure 4 (boxplot); those images could not be fetched from this environment (network access is restricted to eutils.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov; www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov, pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov and mdpi.com all refused or redirected). Left threshold_mmol_per_l empty rather than estimate a number from an unreadable log-scale figure. The paper does report, in Section 4 Discussion, that AVI/AVI non-tasters separate from other genotypes at approximately 0.3-0.6 mM (a separation boundary, not a per-group mean) and that PAV/PAV and PAV/AVI are on average tens to fifty times more sensitive than AVI/AVI; the QUEST procedure tested concentrations from 0.002 to 3.2 mM (=mmol/L) in 20 log-spaced steps." .

<https://w3id.org/hto/data/published/group/PAV_PAV> rdfs:label "adults in Japan, PROP taste testing cohort (Aoki et al. 2023, Nutrients 15(10):2415), TAS2R38 PAV/PAV (n=24)" ;
    obo:HTO_0000058 <https://w3id.org/hto/data/published/diplotype/PAV_PAV> ;
    dcterms:source "PMID:37242298" ;
    rdfs:comment "Aoki et al. 2023, doi:10.3390/nu15102415, PMC10222862 (open access; full text fetched and read via eutils efetch db=pmc). Table 2 reports n=24 for PAV/PAV among the 79 subjects who completed PROP taste detection testing by the modified 2AFC procedure with the QUEST method. Section 3.2 (Results) reports the TAS2R38 genotype effect on threshold was significant (one-way ANOVA F(2,76)=163.32, MSE=12.940, p<0.001) and that PAV/PAV differed significantly from AVI/AVI (p<0.001) and from PAV/AVI (p<0.01), but no numeric per-genotype mean/median threshold (in mM or otherwise) is stated anywhere in the article text or its two tables (Table 1: SNP/genotype frequencies; Table 2: demographics only). The only per-genotype threshold values in the paper are shown graphically, on a log scale, in Figure 3 (scatter plot) and Figure 4 (boxplot); those images could not be fetched from this environment (network access is restricted to eutils.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov; www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov, pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov and mdpi.com all refused or redirected). Left threshold_mmol_per_l empty rather than estimate a number from an unreadable log-scale figure. The paper does report, in Section 4 Discussion, that AVI/AVI non-tasters separate from other genotypes at approximately 0.3-0.6 mM (a separation boundary, not a per-group mean) and that PAV/PAV and PAV/AVI are on average tens to fifty times more sensitive than AVI/AVI; the QUEST procedure tested concentrations from 0.002 to 3.2 mM (=mmol/L) in 20 log-spaced steps." .

